# 
\title{L09.1 Three Essential Computer Vision Tasks}
\subtitle{CSci 560: Deep Learning w/ Python (Chollet) Ch. 9.1}
\maketitle

# The Three Main Computer Vision Tasks

::: columns
::: {.column width=60%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.15]{figures/ch09-1-three-computer-vision-tasks}
\label{fig:ch09-1-three-computer-vision-tasks}
\caption{The three main comuter vision tasks: classification, segmentation, detection. \parencite[pg.239]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
- **Image classification** goal is to assign one or more labels to whole image.
- **Image segmentation** goal is to "segment" or "partition" an image into different areas.
- **Object detection** goal is to draw **bounding boxes** around objects of interest and classify.
::::
:::


# 
\title{L09.2 An Image Segmentation Example}
\subtitle{CSci 560: Deep Learning w/ Python (Chollet) Ch. 9.2}
\maketitle

# Semantic vs. Instance Segmentation

::: columns
::: {.column width=60%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.15]{figures/ch09-2-semantic-instance-segmentation}
\label{fig:ch09-2-semantic-instance-segmentation}
\caption{Semantic segmentation vs. instance segmentation. \parencite[pg.241]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
- **Semantic segmentation** each pixel is independently classified.
- **Instance segmentation** seeks not only to classify image pixels by category, but also to parse out individual object instances.
::::
:::


